  "platform": "Sega Mega Drive / Genesis",
  "summary": "Light Crusader is Treasure's action adventure, first released for Mega Drive in Japan on 26 May 1995.",
  "body": [
    "Light Crusader is Treasure's action adventure, first released for Mega Drive in Japan on 26 May 1995. Sir David arrives in Green Row, where inhabitants have been disappearing, and enters the dungeon beneath the town to investigate. Its rooms use an isometric view, giving platforms, movable objects and passages a visible sense of depth. David fights with a sword, but reaching the next chamber often requires understanding how the space works. The town above provides a place to speak to people and buy supplies between expeditions below.",
    "David can run, jump and attack while standing or moving, so the dungeon asks the player to judge position as well as choose a direction. Objects can be moved into useful places, and the arrangement of a room often provides the clues to its solution. Isometric depth makes a gap or ledge less immediately obvious than it would be in a side view. Reaching it successfully comes from connecting the visible layout with David's movement, then carrying out that short sequence while enemies or hazards occupy the same space.",
    "Magic is organised around fire, water, wind and earth. Combining the elements produces different spells, while water also supplies a way to restore health. The system gives the collected elements a purpose beyond a single fixed attack, inviting experimentation when a room or enemy becomes troublesome. Light Crusader was Treasure's final Mega Drive release, and its construction differs markedly from the studio's faster shooting games. Much of its appeal comes from the small discovery inside a chamber: a useful combination, a movable obstacle or a route that becomes clear once viewed from another angle."
  ],
  "story_headings": [
    "Below Green Row",
    "Moving through a puzzle",
    "Four elements to combine"
  ],
